{
 "read": "4 November 2026, from OpenRouter's public endpoint list for each pin (https://openrouter.ai/api/v1/models/<pin>/endpoints), with no key and no model call",
 "chosen": {
  "google/gemini-4-argon": "google-vertex/global",
  "qwen/qwen3.8-max-0902": "alibaba",
  "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5": "anthropic"
 },
 "routes": {
  "google/gemini-4-argon": {
   "order": [
    "google-vertex/global"
   ],
   "ignore": [
    "google-vertex/global/priority"
   ]
  },
  "qwen/qwen3.8-max-0902": {
   "order": [
    "alibaba"
   ],
   "ignore": [
    "alibaba/us-east-1"
   ]
  },
  "anthropic/claude-sonnet-5.5": {
   "order": [
    "anthropic"
   ]
  }
 },
 "routing_rule": {
  "read": "4 November 2026, OpenRouter's public provider-routing and service-tier pages, no key, no call",
  "pages": [
   "https://openrouter.ai/docs/features/provider-routing",
   "https://openrouter.ai/docs/guides/features/service-tiers.md",
   "https://openrouter.ai/docs/api/api-reference/chat/create-a-chat-completion.md",
   "https://openrouter.ai/docs/api-reference/get-a-generation"
  ],
  "as_read": [
   "A slug has the form provider[/variant][/region][/tier]. In order, only and ignore, a slug names every eligible endpoint that shares it as a prefix, so a base slug names all of a provider's regions and variants; dropping the tier never names a tier endpoint, and a base slug does not by itself opt into a service tier.",
   "The pages also say that priority endpoints of open-weight models already in the routing pool match their base slug; none of the three pins is open-weight, and each route ignores by name every tier endpoint below its slug, so the route names one endpoint under either reading.",
   "allow_fallbacks false with an order serves a request only by the providers listed; require_parameters true routes only to an endpoint that takes every parameter sent.",
   "A response reports its tier in service_tier (default, flex, priority, ultrafast, or null). The documented chat-completion schema lists no provider field; the run reads the response's top-level provider, and a response that names none stops the smoke run before any scored call.",
   "Where a response names no provider, OpenRouter documents a generation record for it, GET /api/v1/generation?id= with the response's id, whose data.provider_name is the name of the provider that served the request and data.service_tier the tier the provider reported, or null; read on 4 November 2026 with no key. An addendum may read the provider and the tier there, one more request per call and no model call."
  ]
 },
 "why": "one endpoint per pin, so that every call of a pin goes to one serving endpoint: the model's own provider's standard endpoint, not a flex or priority tier, not another host, and not one that lists implicit caching, since a cache hit or miss is a serving difference among identical requests. For Gemini, google-vertex/global, named by its full slug, its priority tier ignored by name. For Qwen, the base alibaba endpoint: its other Alibaba endpoint, alibaba/us-east-1, lists implicit caching, and the base slug alone would name both, so the route orders alibaba and ignores alibaba/us-east-1. For Sonnet, reported apart, anthropic, its one Anthropic endpoint, a base slug that would also name an Anthropic endpoint added later. A response names its provider and its tier, which the run checks on every call; its region and caching cannot be seen from a response.",
 "lists": {
